title The gate that counts the record -- two gates disagree about a parked lap
prompt Autonomous lap. The card left tally/README.md at C+ 77 as my frame and %589 as Keaton's word.
rota lap 4297, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees. Threshold page first, then Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. What the reading surfaced: look hard before cutting, and cut once at exactly the depth the truth requires. That is why this lap read three stashes' CODE instead of restoring them, and why it ends having landed nothing and dropped nothing.
think Cold roster first, held still while it ran: 190 guards, 185 green, 2 red, 3 gated. stash_record red with unlanded=3; standing_equipment red reporting it as roster_broken. Reds first, so the tally/README frame waits.
think Three round-open stashes of this seat hold a session log nothing else carries: two in stash@{0}, one in stash@{4}. I read each lap's own log, then its code, which is where the reading turned.
think stash@{4} is a finished GREEN lap that made Weave.apply binary search self.lines.items, declaring identity order a Weave invariant. It is superseded and now unsafe: 8fb075741 -- REDS %599, from a DIFFERENT parked lap eight hours later -- repaired the same red by sorting the caller's diff.deletes into a scratch slice and walking the weave once, explicitly because merge returns its union in place order (bdb20178b).
think git merge-file against stash@{4}^ returned four conflicts whose first two carry the opposite claims in their own comments. A clean apply would have printed nothing, which is the part worth keeping: a stash carries no premise, so recoverability is not a property a stash can have.
think Landed the three logs as testimony, each with a landing field naming what did not land beside it. The hot pass then answered log_file_claim red, verdict=staged_claim_unwritten, staged_unwritten=8, naming the eight paths those logs claim.
think That refusal is the finding rather than an obstacle to it. A parked lap's file fields name the files parked beside it, so stash_record and log_file_claim cannot both be green over a parked lap while its work stays in the stash. Both gates are right.
think So I tried the other closing move -- land the work. Recovered rish_report_bound from the stash tree with git checkout, modes riding, and proved it on today's tree before touching a shared file: scan sites=38 files=26 ceiling=38 under_ceiling, control pass=15 fail=0, witness GREEN.
think Then read the roster it wanted to join, and found say_compose_bound seated 20260908.014200 under %592 -- the same 4,096-byte StrBuf hazard, read off Rishi's own declaration, across 2,422 scripts and 3,281 shaped sites, gating shares in per mille where the parked guard narrows to 38 sites under a ceiling on the count. Its successor's own comment rejects exactly that shape, because a count ceiling reds on the most ordinary act on this pier.
think Withdrew it. A third guard reading a subset of a rostered guard's population, at 19s a lap, is a cost with no reading behind it -- and the withdrawal judges duplication rather than health, which is why I proved it green first.
think Booked the row at %632 read from the anointed spine after a fetch. It is OPEN, so door B keeps it on the pin; the pin stood at 40,430 of 40,960 with only %623 BOOKED, so I folded %623 to make room.
obs The lap I planned is not the lap I ran, and the tree is what corrected me twice. First when a guard refused the record I was landing, and again when a roster comment named the guard I was about to duplicate. Both refusals were cheaper than the work they stopped.
obs A record and the work it records are one unit. Where the work still wants landing, stash_record is exactly right and the move is to land both. Where the tree has healed around it there is no lawful move at all: log_file_claim refuses the log, and the only remaining act is to drop the stash, which is a cut and stays Keaton's word.
obs That makes stash_record's unlanded a gate no lap can satisfy for this class. It is %626's shape read from the other side, with %463 beside it, since orphans_work=6 is reported and says nothing about the half that decides.
obs I applied no parked code, dropped no stash, and rewrote no parked log. The three logs were staged, refused, and withdrawn whole; the index rows and the day count went back with them.
obs The rebase renumbered the row %632 -> %636 and took my own %623 fold out from under me: a peer had folded that same row to REDS-a-seat-named-in-four-places-rows-623.md at 20260908.094425 while this lap ran. My shelf was the duplicate, so it went; the stamp is the key and the number is a view, which is derived-spine working twice in one send.
obs My own frame is still open and I am saying so rather than quietly closing it: tally/README.md reads C+ 77, its reach 60 on a Flesch-Kincaid grade of 13 against the Door ceiling of 9. A red preempted it, which is reds-first working.
